Subject: [PATCH] dwc2: bring host mode up after a single IDDIG debounce on role-switch platforms
Upstream-Status: Pending

An accessory-initiated role switch on the Carlinkit Mini Ultra (Ingenic
X1600, DWC2 core 3.00a, IDDIG filter present and no bypass) costs two
debounce periods before the root hub port is powered:

  [1.006232] force_mode(host): GUSBCFG 4000140f -> 2000140f
  [1.106381] dwc2_conn_id_status_change(): GOTGCTL=0024007c    <- +100ms, core is host
  [1.106448] wait_for_mode(host): start GINTSTS=14000020        <- core reset, device again
  [1.206952] wait_for_mode(host): done after 100 ms              <- +200ms
  [1.207129] Init: Port Power applied, HPRT0=00001000

The first period is the hardware filter reacting to the force-mode
write. The second is dwc2_conn_id_status_change() calling
dwc2_core_init(false), which soft-resets the core, and as
dwc2_core_reset() documents, a soft reset puts the core back into device
mode until the filter has run once more.

Whether a head unit tolerates that decides catplay-firmware issue #1. A
Continental NAC Wave 2 (Toyota Proace Verso) does not. With the vendor
firmware, which forces host mode 18ms after the role-switch request and
has its gadget disabled 100ms after that, the head unit is enumerated
as a device 300ms later. With the stock flow the port is powered ~200ms
after the force and no device is ever seen on it: 24 HPRT0 samples over
six seconds, CONNSTS=0 in every one, in every capture, and when the
dongle returns to device mode the head unit re-enumerates it at once,
so it had long gone back to host mode. The maintainer's reading of the
issue is the same: "role switch timings being again on the edge of what
car tolerates", "another 100ms to cut in dwc2.c".

Cut it by folding the two debounce periods into one. On role-switch
platforms (dr_mode OTG with a usb-role-switch property, the condition
patches 0039 and 0043 key on):

 * dwc2_drd_role_sw_set() soft-resets the core itself, right after the
   force-mode write, with skip_wait so it does not block. The debounce
   period the reset restarts is then the one the force-mode write costs
   anyway. Force-mode bits and GOTGCTL session overrides survive the
   reset. Since the reset also clears GAHBCFG.GLBL_INTR_EN and GINTMSK,
   no Connector ID Status Change interrupt will follow; the handler is
   queued directly instead.

 * dwc2_conn_id_status_change() decides A/B on the GUSBCFG force bits
   rather than the filtered connector ID, which still reads B when the
   work runs; polls for host mode at 1-2ms instead of 20ms; and then
   calls dwc2_core_init(hsotg, true), i.e. without a second reset.
   initial_setup=true also passes select_phy=true to dwc2_phy_init(),
   and dwc2_hs_phy_init() only writes GUSBCFG (and only then resets) if
   the PHY selection differs from what is programmed - PHYIF16 survives
   the reset, so it does not.

History of this patch, because each version taught something:

v1 only skipped the reset in dwc2_conn_id_status_change() and did no
reset at all. Bench: port powered 100.3ms after the force. Car, three
role switches in one boot: the head unit pulled up D+ 32-36ms after
port power every time, was reset and enabled at high speed, got "new
high-speed USB device number 2 using dwc2" - and then enumeration went
nowhere. No descriptor, no error, the head unit dropped the port 3.7s
later. A mode switch without a core reset leaves the transfer side in a
state the databook does not define; that is what the mainline reset is
for.

v2 moved the reset to dwc2_drd_role_sw_set() as described above. Bench:
identical timing to v1. Car: the head unit never attached again, same
as before the patch. Same time to port power as v1, the only difference
being the ~100ms the core spends in device mode right after the reset.
And that window is not idle: DCTL's reset value is "connected", which
is why dwc2_gadget_init() sets SftDiscon straight after the probe-time
reset. So v2 presented a pull-up again within microseconds of dropping
it, for ~100ms, and the head unit - still a host - saw disconnect,
reconnect, disconnect where it expects one disconnect before it swaps
roles. The stock flow does the same thing 100ms later. v1 never reset,
never re-presented, and is the only variant that got the head unit on
the bus.

v3 keeps v2 and sets SftDiscon before the force-mode write (one clean
disconnect at a moment we choose) and again right after the reset (so
the debounce window has no pull-up). DCTL is logged after the reset.

Expected on this hardware: "Init: Port Power applied" ~100ms after
force_mode(host), preceded by "role switch: core reset folded into
force-mode debounce ... DCTL=00000002" and "connId A: host mode after
~100 ms", then "Port Connect Detected" within tens of ms as in v1, and
then - the part v1 never got to - "usb 1-1: New USB device found".

Written against the letux-6.18.y tree at a3d00c045d89 with 0021, 0022,
0030, 0039, 0043 and 0044 applied. v2 bench-verified 2026-09-15 (port
powered 100.4ms after the force, B path back to device mode intact,
interrupts counting). v3 not yet compiled or run.
---
 drivers/usb/dwc2/drd.c | 69 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 drivers/usb/dwc2/hcd.c | 69 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++--------
 2 files changed, 129 insertions(+), 9 deletions(-)

--- a/drivers/usb/dwc2/drd.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/dwc2/drd.c
@@ -83,6 +83,75 @@
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/*
+ * Device -> host on a role-switch platform, stock flow:
+ *
+ *   force-mode write ... IDDIG debounce (100ms) ... ConIDStsChng IRQ
+ *   -> dwc2_conn_id_status_change() -> dwc2_core_init(false)
+ *   -> core soft reset -> core drops back to device mode
+ *   ... IDDIG debounce (100ms) again ... -> dwc2_hcd_start()
+ *
+ * Two debounce periods before the root hub port is powered, and only the
+ * second one is a real requirement: mainline resets the core on every mode
+ * change so host init starts from a known state, and the filter restarts on
+ * every reset. Fold them: reset the core right here, immediately after the
+ * force-mode write, so the debounce period the reset restarts is the one
+ * the force-mode write costs anyway. The force-mode bits and the GOTGCTL
+ * session overrides survive a soft reset (see dwc2_core_reset() and
+ * dwc2_ovr_avalid()), so nothing about the requested mode is lost.
+ *
+ * The reset also clears GAHBCFG.GLBL_INTR_EN and GINTMSK, so no Connector
+ * ID Status Change interrupt is going to announce the mode change. Queue
+ * the handler ourselves: it polls for host mode, skips its own reset on
+ * this platform and brings the HCD up - ~100ms after the force-mode write
+ * instead of ~200ms.
+ *
+ * Only ever called with role == USB_ROLE_HOST, dr_mode == OTG and the HCD
+ * initialised (wq_otg exists).
+ */
+static void dwc2_drd_early_host_reset(struct dwc2_hsotg *hsotg)
+{
+	unsigned long flags;
+	int ret;
+
+	/*
+	 * dwc2_conn_id_status_change() would do this ~100ms from now. The
+	 * gadget side may as well learn about the disconnect before the
+	 * reset wipes the controller state under it.
+	 */
+	spin_lock_irqsave(&hsotg->lock, flags);
+	dwc2_hsotg_disconnect(hsotg);
+	/* One clean disconnect for the head unit, at a moment we choose. */
+	dwc2_hsotg_core_disconnect(hsotg);
+	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&hsotg->lock, flags);
+
+	ret = dwc2_core_reset(hsotg, true);
+	if (ret)
+		dev_err(hsotg->dev,
+			"role switch: early core reset failed: %d\n", ret);
+
+	/*
+	 * For one debounce period after the reset the core sits in device
+	 * mode with DCTL at its reset value, and that value is "connected":
+	 * dwc2_gadget_init() has to set SftDiscon right after the probe-time
+	 * reset for exactly that reason ("Be in disconnected state until
+	 * gadget is registered"). Left alone, the core re-presents a pull-up
+	 * for ~100ms right after we dropped it - and the head unit, still a
+	 * host at this point, sees disconnect / reconnect / disconnect where
+	 * it expects a single disconnect before it swaps roles itself. Car
+	 * captures: the head unit attaches as a device only in the one
+	 * variant of this patch that did not reset the core at all.
+	 */
+	dwc2_hsotg_core_disconnect(hsotg);
+
+	dev_info(hsotg->dev,
+		 "role switch: core reset folded into force-mode debounce, GOTGCTL=%08x GUSBCFG=%08x GINTSTS=%08x DCTL=%08x\n",
+		 dwc2_readl(hsotg, GOTGCTL), dwc2_readl(hsotg, GUSBCFG),
+		 dwc2_readl(hsotg, GINTSTS), dwc2_readl(hsotg, DCTL));
+
+	queue_work(hsotg->wq_otg, &hsotg->wf_otg);
+}
+
 static int dwc2_drd_role_sw_set(struct usb_role_switch *sw, enum usb_role role)
 {
 	struct dwc2_hsotg *hsotg = usb_role_switch_get_drvdata(sw);
@@ -187,10 +256,15 @@
 
 	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&hsotg->lock, flags);
 
-	if (!already && hsotg->dr_mode == USB_DR_MODE_OTG)
+	if (!already && hsotg->dr_mode == USB_DR_MODE_OTG) {
 		/* This will raise a Connector ID Status Change Interrupt */
 		dwc2_force_mode(hsotg, role == USB_ROLE_HOST);
 
+		/* ...except that for host we do not wait for it, see above. */
+		if (role == USB_ROLE_HOST && hsotg->wq_otg)
+			dwc2_drd_early_host_reset(hsotg);
+	}
+
 	if (!hsotg->ll_hw_enabled && hsotg->clk)
 		clk_disable_unprepare(hsotg->clk);
 
--- a/drivers/usb/dwc2/hcd.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/dwc2/hcd.c
@@ -3151,11 +3151,32 @@
 	u32 count = 0;
 	u32 gotgctl;
 	unsigned long flags;
+	ktime_t start;
 
 	gotgctl = dwc2_readl(hsotg, GOTGCTL);
 	dev_info(hsotg->dev, "%s(): GOTGCTL=%08x conidsts=%d\n", __func__,
 		 gotgctl, !!(gotgctl & GOTGCTL_CONID_B));
 
+	/*
+	 * On role-switch platforms the mode is dictated by the GUSBCFG force
+	 * bits, and the connector ID in GOTGCTL merely follows them - one
+	 * IDDIG debounce period later, and from device mode all over again
+	 * after every core soft reset. dwc2_drd_role_sw_set() queues this
+	 * work right after forcing host mode and resetting the core, i.e.
+	 * while the filtered ID still reads B. Decide on the force bits, so
+	 * the A path below gets to wait for host mode instead of the B path
+	 * happily re-initialising device mode.
+	 */
+	if (hsotg->dr_mode == USB_DR_MODE_OTG &&
+	    device_property_read_bool(hsotg->dev, "usb-role-switch")) {
+		u32 gusbcfg = dwc2_readl(hsotg, GUSBCFG);
+
+		if (gusbcfg & GUSBCFG_FORCEHOSTMODE)
+			gotgctl &= ~GOTGCTL_CONID_B;
+		else if (gusbcfg & GUSBCFG_FORCEDEVMODE)
+			gotgctl |= GOTGCTL_CONID_B;
+	}
+
 	/* B-Device connector (Device Mode) */
 	if (gotgctl & GOTGCTL_CONID_B) {
 		dwc2_vbus_supply_exit(hsotg);
@@ -3209,25 +3230,60 @@
 		/* A-Device connector (Host Mode) */
 		dev_info(hsotg->dev, "connId A (GOTGCTL=%08x)\n",
 			 dwc2_readl(hsotg, GOTGCTL));
+		/*
+		 * Poll at 1-2ms rather than 20ms: on role-switch platforms
+		 * this is the wait for the IDDIG debounce period restarted by
+		 * the early reset in dwc2_drd_role_sw_set(), and every
+		 * millisecond here is a millisecond the head unit sits as a
+		 * device without a host. Same ~5s budget as before.
+		 */
+		start = ktime_get();
 		while (!dwc2_is_host_mode(hsotg)) {
-			dev_info(hsotg->dev, "Waiting for Host Mode, Mode=%s\n",
-				 dwc2_is_host_mode(hsotg) ?
-				 "Host" : "Peripheral");
-			msleep(20);
-			if (++count > 250)
+			usleep_range(1000, 2000);
+			if (++count > 2500)
 				break;
 		}
-		if (count > 250)
+		if (count > 2500)
 			dev_err(hsotg->dev,
 				"Connection id status change timed out\n");
+		else if (count)
+			dev_info(hsotg->dev,
+				 "connId A: host mode after %lld ms (%u polls)\n",
+				 ktime_ms_delta(ktime_get(), start), count);
 
 		spin_lock_irqsave(&hsotg->lock, flags);
 		dwc2_hsotg_disconnect(hsotg);
 		spin_unlock_irqrestore(&hsotg->lock, flags);
 
 		hsotg->op_state = OTG_STATE_A_HOST;
-		/* Initialize the Core for Host mode */
-		dwc2_core_init(hsotg, false);
+
+		/*
+		 * Initialize the Core for Host mode.
+		 *
+		 * dwc2_core_init(hsotg, false) soft-resets the core, and per
+		 * dwc2_core_reset() a soft reset drops the core back into
+		 * device mode until the IDDIG debounce filter has run once
+		 * more - 100ms on cores older than 3.10a, which have no
+		 * filter bypass. On role-switch platforms that reset has
+		 * already been done by dwc2_drd_role_sw_set(), right after
+		 * the force-mode write, so that its debounce period and the
+		 * one the force-mode write costs anyway are the same period.
+		 * Resetting again here would pay it twice, which is exactly
+		 * the ~100ms a NAC Wave 2 does not wait for.
+		 *
+		 * initial_setup=true is "do not reset, (re)select the PHY":
+		 * dwc2_hs_phy_init() only touches GUSBCFG (and only then
+		 * resets) if the PHY selection differs from what is already
+		 * programmed, which it never does at this point.
+		 */
+		if (hsotg->dr_mode == USB_DR_MODE_OTG &&
+		    device_property_read_bool(hsotg->dev, "usb-role-switch")) {
+			dev_info(hsotg->dev,
+				 "connId A: role-switch platform, host init without second core reset\n");
+			dwc2_core_init(hsotg, true);
+		} else {
+			dwc2_core_init(hsotg, false);
+		}
 		dwc2_enable_global_interrupts(hsotg);
 		dev_info(hsotg->dev,
 			 "connId A: core reinit done, mode=%s, starting HCD\n",
